ce and Rome was carried out by the Pandavas in the Mahabarata. I haven't read it myself, but it's not a very long story, as I understand it. From the way it's been described to me, the Pandavas literally just waltzed into Greece and Rome because they were bored. They conquered the whole place and then just left, not even bothering to leave behind a puppet regime or an occupation force.<br /> <br /> \u200bLula described the story in a Scion perspective by using it as an example of what this &quot;All Myths Are True&quot; World looks like. The Pandavas, in the Scion World, knew full-well they'd never hold Greece and Rome as it would only be a matter of time before the Theoi retaliated against them, so they just left.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> &quot;We're bored out of our minds... let's take down an empire!&quot; <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-27T04:41:08-07:00","id":1153437,"title":"","content":"Watcher,I've got a questions if you don't mind. In your games Canon,do the Titans free themselves at some point? <br /> Would you say Kvasir is a God in your games Canon?<br /> Are the dead Tuatha just Gone,or are their souls in their heaven? Could they reincarnate?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4621-nicolas-milioni","name":"Nicolas Milioni","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4621_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3605"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-27T06:17:00-07:00","id":1153454,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1151822-all-myths-are-true-my-cosmology?p=1153413#post1153413\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> <br /> &quot;We're bored out of our minds... let's take down an empire!&quot; <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>\u200bSounds like sufficient motivation for a Scion adventure to me! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">&quot;We have done the impossible, and that makes us mighty.&quot;<br /> Captain Malcolm Reynolds</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/679-unnatural1","name":"unnatural1","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-27T07:05:42-07:00","id":1153466,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nicolas Milioni</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1151822-all-myths-are-true-my-cosmology?p=1153437#post1153437\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Watcher,I've got a questions if you don't mind. In your games Canon,do the Titans free themselves at some point?<br /> Would you say Kvasir is a God in your games Canon?<br /> Are the dead Tuatha just Gone,or are their souls in their heaven? Could they reincarnate?</div> </div> </div> </div>I absolutely don't mind, I'm more than happy to answer things.<br /> <br /> Not all Titans could free themselves in my cosmology, but some individuals might be able to. Fenrir, for example, is Fated to free himself, while on the other hand Lir needs outside influence to be freed since he is entrapped in his grief. Most of the time I like to leave the Divine side of the world static and unchanged for my Players, so any change inflicted on the Divine is the result of their own actions (<i>or</i> inaction) for example.<br /> <br /> Kvasir... good question. I would probably be tempted to make him a Titan for the following reasons. A) He is Sealed, being dead, and consumed by other beings. B) He is a Personification in that the consumption of his being imparts mastery of his field onto people. C) He is of nebulous origin, being claimed as Vanir, Aesir, and Neither at the same time. D) He is more powerful than the Gods in his field, he is wiser and knows more than even Odin.<br /> <br /> If a player was dead set on playing a Scion of Kvasir, I would write it up, but I'd have the Titanic angle as a plot point, and I would suggest Bragi as an option if they wanted a Divine parent along the same lines without that baggage.<br /> <br /> The Tuatha are a trickier question. Lugh fathers CuChulain (one of the three times he is begotten), and heals CuChulain after his death. Some of the Tuatha who are dead flit in and out of later myths, such as those of the Fennian Cycle. However, they are most certainly dead. What I tend to interpret this in Scion as is that the Tuatha seem to be able to leave their Afterlife if they want to. I wouldn't say they are reincarnating though since they're always returning as the same people, it's more like they just wandered home when they felt bothered to do so. <br /> <br /> The reason I make this choice is because if I rule that the dead Tuatha are dead, then we have pretty much no one left. Nuada, and Macha are slain by Balor. Cairpre dies of sunstroke, Ogma died to Indech of the Fomorians, Dian Cecht and Goibniu died to plague, Midir dies to Nuada, Lugh dies to Mac Cuill, Aengus is drowned by unknown people, Net, Badb, and Neman died to the Fomori, The Dagda dies to an infected spear wound to the stomach inflicted by Cethlenn of the Crooked Teeth, Eriu dies to Suirge, Fotla dies to Etain, for the big names in the very least.<br /> <br /> So, what we would be left with is Manannan mac Lir, Brigid, and one third of The Morrigu. (The Morrigan survives, where Macha, Neman, and Badb all die)<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-27T08:11:43-07:00","id":1153477,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Watcher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1151822-all-myths-are-true-my-cosmology?p=1153466#post1153466\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I absolutely don't mind, I'm more than happy to answer things.<br /> <br /> Not all Titans could free themselves in my cosmology, but some individuals might be able to. Fenrir, for example, is Fated to free himself, while on the other hand Lir needs outside influence to be freed since he is entrapped in his grief. Most of the time I like to leave the Divine side of the world static and unchanged for my Players, so any change inflicted on the Divine is the result of their own actions (<i>or</i> inaction) for example.<br /> <br /> Kvasir... good question. I would probably be tempted to make him a Titan for the following reasons. A) He is Sealed, being dead, and consumed by other beings. B) He is a Personification in that the consumption of his being imparts mastery of his field onto people. C) He is of nebulous origin, being claimed as Vanir, Aesir, and Neither at the same time. D) He is more powerful than the Gods in his field, he is wiser and knows more than even Odin.<br /> <br /> If a player was dead set on playing a Scion of Kvasir, I would write it up, but I'd have the Titanic angle as a plot point, and I would suggest Bragi as an option if they wanted a Divine parent along the same lines without that baggage.<br /> <br /> The Tuatha are a trickier question. Lugh fathers CuChulain (one of the three times he is begotten), and heals CuChulain after his death. Some of the Tuatha who are dead flit in and out of later myths, such as those of the Fennian Cycle. However, they are most certainly dead. What I tend to interpret this in Scion as is that the Tuatha seem to be able to leave their Afterlife if they want to. I wouldn't say they are reincarnating though since they're always returning as the same people, it's more like they just wandered home when they felt bothered to do so.<br /> <br /> The reason I make this choice is because if I rule that the dead Tuatha are dead, then we have pretty much no one left. Nuada, and Macha are slain by Balor. Cairpre dies of sunstroke, Ogma died to Indech of the Fomorians, Dian Cecht and Goibniu died to plague, Midir dies to Nuada, Lugh dies to Mac Cuill, Aengus is drowned by unknown people, Net, Badb, and Neman died to the Fomori, The Dagda dies to an infected spear wound to the stomach inflicted by Cethlenn of the Crooked Teeth, Eriu dies to Suirge, Fotla dies to Etain, for the big names in the very least.<br /> <br /> So, what we would be left with is Manannan mac Lir, Brigid, and one third of The Morrigu. (The Morrigan survives, where Macha, Neman, and Badb all die)</div> </div> </div> </div>On a book i read,by rick riordan, it was said the Vanir and the Aesir all spit into a cup and Kvasir was magically born because of that,he was described as Living Peace Treaty,do you think it was accurate?<br /> One thing i didnt understand in the scion 2e version of the tuatha,is that assumption they retired to the overworld and became the gods of ireland instead of living in ireland,is that any anything in the original literature to base that assumtion?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4621-nicolas-milioni","name":"Nicolas Milioni","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4621_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3605"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-27T09:44:35-07:00","id":1153508,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nicolas Milioni</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1151822-all-myths-are-true-my-cosmology?p=1153477#post1153477\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">On a book i read,by rick riordan, it was said the Vanir and the Aesir all spit into a cup and Kvasir was magically born because of that,he was described as Living Peace Treaty,do you think it was accurate?</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think that is a baseless interpretation of the text, but there are some details that might make it confusing. Kvasir shows up in multiple sagas, and his exact identity shifts slightly to fit the narrative at hand. Sometimes he is Aesir, sometimes he is a Vanir taken hostage, sometimes he is this strange entity created from both Pantheons. I wouldn't necessarily say Kvasir is a living peace treaty, but I would say that he is deeply linked with notions of meetings, and discussion. He is connected to mead, and poetry, two things that would be very important for any sort of diplomatic function for the Norse. Mead functioning as shorthand for Hospitality in general, offering up your abundance to your guest, and the skaldic poetry as a form of entertainment that would be provided at such meetings.<br /> <br /> So, I don't think the interpretation of him as a Living Peace Treaty is wrong, but I think it might cause confusion when looking at all of the texts rather than just one. Riordan does seem to be on the right track that Kvasir has a connection with notions of discussion, and social relationships however. Peace Treaty would be slightly too strong a word in my mind.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nicolas Milioni</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1151822-all-myths-are-true-my-cosmology?p=1153477#post1153477\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One thing i didnt understand in the scion 2e version of the tuatha,is that assumption they retired to the overworld and became the gods of ireland instead of living in ireland,is that any anything in the original literature to base that assumtion?</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh yeah, it's the basis of Irish mythology. Or, well sort of. The Tuatha didn't claim the Overworld, they don't actually have a notion of an 'Up-Place' or 'Sky-Place' as far as we can find. Though there are some who argue for a triplicate division of the world into Above, Surface, Beneath, but I have not read an argument of such which I buy yet. I think that might be the result of Greco-Roman dominance of our vision of cosmologies. However, the Tuatha <i>did</i> claim the <i>Otherworld</i> which is... how to explain it.<br /> <br /> The Otherworld is Here-But-Not-Here. If you know Changeling: The Lost, it is Arcadia and The Hedge (though for some reason Changeling: The Lost 1e refused to ever talk about Ireland despite a majority of their core concepts being Irish in origin, it's a <i>thing</i> that bugs me. I am hoping Changeling 2e addresses it). It is a place of wonderous beauty, except it is <i>very dangerous</i>, and while the basic idea of the world is all there, trees, birds, people, they're not <i>right</i>. Birds with iron beaks and golden wings. Birds whose songs wake the dead. Trees of crystal, with fruit that can feed three hundred warriors. People who are Beautiful, or Terrifying, sometimes both, and never just 'normal'. No capacity for anything that is in the middle ground, everything is <i>extreme</i> in every aspect.<br /> <br /> This Otherworld is the Godrealm of the Tuatha, more or less. Or maybe Godrealms. It's complicated. They are beneath Ireland instead of on it.<br /> <br /> When the Sons of Mil arrive, they defeat the Tribes of the Goddess Danu, and Ireland is divided in two. Humanity is given the surface of Ireland, and the Divine are given the underground, and so they retreat into the S\u00eddhe, the neolithic / bronze age burial mounds of Ireland to a strange, fantastic location (or locations, it isn't clear in the texts) beneath the earth. This is the Otherworld. Depending on which of the two versions of the story, either The Dagda dispenses out all of his lands to the individual members of the Tribe, or Manannan mac Lir arrives to form the peace, and divides the land.<br /> <br /> They lived in Ireland once, before the coming of humanity, but they were defeated and driven underground. Generally the notion of 'down there' is used for them, be it at the bottom of wells, beneath the hills, or beneath the sea. The idea of the Otherworldly Islands is well-argued to actually be a later Christian alteration of the cosmology as the scribes struggled to acomodate both the presence of the Tuatha as well as Christianity, for some reason not demonizing the pre-Christian Gods. However, even though I am a stickler for accuracy, at the moment I use the Otherworldly Islands for some of the Tuatha Terra Ingocnita. I might change it one day when I sit down and really dig into the topic for one of my games.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","user":"Watcher","date":". 10-27-2017, 12:46 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count"